{We're concluding our analysis of the a3 Sicilian with 2...g6. In this study, we will be taking a look at how to respond if Black chooses to set up with 5...d6.}
1. e4 c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play a3.} 2. a3 g6 {The early ...g6 is a favorite of Sicilian Dragon players. Let's go for Nc3 as recommended. [%cal Gf8g7]} 3. Nc3 Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll develop the bishop next.} 4. Bc4 Nc6 {Black naturally develops their knight to c6. We'll support the light squares in the center and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 5. d3 d6 {Black plays ...d6 now, opening up the light-squared bishop. This move is third in popularity and it's played 20% of the time. Against 5...d6, we will also continue in "Vienna" style with f4, Nf3 and O-O.} 6. f4 e6 {20% of the time, Black plays ...e6 in order to develop their knight to e7 and prepare a central expansion with ...d5. We continue with our plan by developing the knight behind the f4-pawn. [%cal Rd6d5]} (6... Nf6 {Nf6 is actually the most common move, but this is simply a transposition to the next study. It's played 58% of the time. Refer to Study 3, variation 6...d6.}) 7. Nf3 Nge7 {Black develops their knight to e7, preparing ...O-O and supporting the potential central thrust. The idea of Ba2 keeps repeating itself! [%cal Gd6d5]} 8. Ba2 O-O {Black continues by castling kingside. Surprisingly enough, this is an inaccuracy and allows us to kickstart an attack! We strike with h4, aiming to play h5 and attack Black's kingside.} (8... d5 {Black immediately strikes in the center with ...d5, which is the critical choice. We'll continue by castling kingside. If Black now captures on e4, we should recapture with the d-pawn. [%csl Re4]} 9. O-O O-O {Black also castles their king.} 10. e5 {We close the center and the diagonal with e5, preparing our plan with Ne2 and Qe1. Notable is that after Ne2 and Qe1, any Black knight on f5 can be kicked out with g4. If Black quickly tries to "break out" with ...f6, we should capture and continue with our plan. [%cal Gc3e2,Gd1e1] [%csl Ge2,Ge1]}) 9. h4 h5 {Most common is ...h5. We already know the downsides of this move, though. That's why we immediately jump into g5.} (9... d5 {The best way for Black to fight against h4 and the oncoming attack is to immediately play in the center with ...d5 and ...Nd4. We continue the attack with h5. We want to capture on g6 and open the h-file. If Black captures on e4, we should recapture with the knight as to keep the queens on the board. [%cal Gc6d4] [%csl Re4]} 10. h5 Nd4 {Black jumps the knight into d4. We shouldn't rush to capture on g6! A better option is Ne2, intending to trade off Black's strong knight on d4. If Black now captures on e4, we should capture on d4 with our f-knight. [%csl Rf3]} 11. Ne2 Nxf3+ {Black captures on f3.} 12. gxf3 {We recapture on f3. White has a slight advantage and great attacking chances with the fascinating Kf2 and Qg1. The idea is to make use of the semi-open g-file and bring as many pieces to the attack as possible. [%cal Ge1f2,Gd1g1] [%csl Gf2,Gg1]}) 10. Ng5 d5 {Black tries to fight back in the center with ...d5. [%csl Re4]} (10... Nd4 {Black jumps to d4. The main idea behind this move is to prepare ...f6 by defending the e6-pawn. [%cal Rf7f6] [%csl Ge6]} 11. Ne2 {We move our knight to e2 in order to trade off the d4-knight and attack Black's kingside with g4. White has a pleasant advantage, a promising attack and great winning chances. [%cal Ge2d4,Gg2g4] [%csl Gg4,Rd4]}) 11. g4 {But we are quicker on the kingside with g4, with a raging kingside attack and an advantage. If Black kicks our knight out with ...f6, we should retreat it to h3. Otherwise, we can consider capturing on h5. [%cal Gg2g4] [%csl Gg4]} *

