{We're continuing our analysis of the a3 Sicilian with 2...g6. In this study, we will be taking a look at how to respond if Black chooses to set up with 5...Nf6.}
1. e4 c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will play a3 to try b4.} 2. a3 g6 {The early ...g6 is a favorite of Sicilian Dragon players. Let's go for Nc3. [%cal Gf8g7]} 3. Nc3 Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We continue with our plan and develop the light-squared bishop to c4.} 4. Bc4 Nc6 {Black naturally develops their knight to c6. We'll connect the pawns.} 5. d3 Nf6 {Black develops the knight to f6 almost 30% of the time. This is slightly less common than 5...e6 and yields different positions. Against ...Nf6, we'll give up on the idea of h4, and instead continue in "Vienna" style.} 6. f4 O-O {Black continues by castling kingside. And we continue with our plan by developing the knight behind the f4-pawn.} (6... d6 {Black can also continue with ...d6, but this quickly transposes. We'll continue with h3 in order to prevent Black's light-squared bishop from developing to g4.} 7. h3 O-O {Black castles their king.} 8. Nf3 {We develop our knight and we've transposed to the 7...d6 lines.}) 7. Nf3 e6 {Slightly more common is ...e6. As we've seen in the previous study, Black plays ...e6 in order to prepare a central expansion with ...d5. We'll implement the Ba2 idea we learned in the previous studies. [%cal Rd7d5]} (7... d6 {Black can also play ...d6, supporting the c-pawn and preparing to develop the light-squared bishop. We should play h3 to take control of the g4-square. [%csl Gc8]} 8. h3 {We develop our knight and we've transposed to the 7...d6 lines.} 8... a6 {In typical Sicilian fashion, Black is preparing to expand on the queenside with ...b5. There is no need to be afraid of the queenside expansion. We can simply continue by castling kingside. [%cal Rb7b5]} (8... e6 {The other option that Black might go for is ...e6, preparing to expand with ...d5. We castle our king in response and let them play ...d5 if they want to. [%cal Rd6d5]} 9. O-O d5 {Black follows up their previous move by expanding.} 10. Ba2 {Interestingly, the engine actually prefers the inclusion of h3 and gives a slight edge to White. Similarly to the other position, we will aim to close the center with e5 and can try to follow it up with Nb5-Nd6. If Black captures on e4, it is important that we recapture with the d-pawn! [%cal Ge4e5,Gc3b5,Gb5d6] [%csl Ge5,Gd6]}) 9. O-O b5 {Black continues with their plan by expanding with ...b5 and attacking our c4-bishop. [%csl Rc4]} 10. Ba2 {The bishop was attacked, so we retreat it to a2. Play can develop in a variety of ways, but some typical attacking ideas include Qe1-Qh4, Ne2 and a timely f5. Be aware of Black pushing ...b4 and the potential ...Qb6+ after the trades on b4. [%cal Gf4f5,Gc3e2,Gd1e1,Ge1h4,Rb5b4] [%csl Gf5,Ge2,Gh4]}) 8. Ba2 d5 {Black follows up their previous move by expanding with ...d5. [%csl Re4]} (8... d6 {Black can also decide to be a bit more "solid" with ...d6, although this leads to a more passive setup.} 9. O-O {Similarly to the main continuation, the computer gives equality, but the position is still rich and complicated. We have interesting attacking ideas involving Qe1-Qh4 and f5. A move like ...d5 is simply a loss of time and we should respond by closing the center with e5. [%cal Gd1e1,Ge1h4,Gf4f5] [%csl Gh4,Gf5]}) 9. O-O {We castle our king. It is pretty hard to suggest some general systematic ideas, but we will aim to close the center with e5 and go for the already mentioned Nb5-Nd6 plan. The computer thinks this position is equal, but I like White's chances. [%cal Ge4e5,Gc3b5,Gb5d6] [%csl Ge5,Gd6]} *

[Chapter "Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5"]
[Study "Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...d5"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/1.e4 Gotham Style Part 2 The Sicilian/04-01 Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 - Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...d5.mp4"]
