{In this chapter, we will be taking a look at how to respond if Black chooses to move their e-pawn on move two. In this particular study, we will be analyzing the position which occurs after Black accepts our gambit and promptly strikes back in the center with ...d5.}
1. e4 c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll continue with a3.} 2. a3 e6 {Black plays ...e6 on move two 17% of the time, and it happened in more than 600,000 games. By playing ...e6, Black has opened up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing ...d5. We will offer a pawn with b4, which is the main idea behind a3. [%cal Gd7d5]} 3. b4 cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. In particular, 60% of the time. We'll recapture.} 4. axb4 Bxb4 {Black decides to take on b4 again almost 90% of the time. We'll kick away Black's bishop with c3. The move c3 is also very beneficial since it will support a central expansion with d4.} (4... d5 {Black can delay recapturing by playing the immediate ...d5, which occurs 4% of the time. We'll respond with e5, closing the center and looking to transpose into the main line. Black might try to continue with ...Nc6, but we can simply reply with d4. [%csl Re4]} 5. e5 Bxb4 {Black captures the pawn on b4 instead, challenging the authority of our gambit. We'll attack Black's bishop with c3 and force it to retreat.} 6. c3 Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to e7.} 7. d4 {We continue with d4, transposing into the main line. [%csl Gd4]}) 5. c3 Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to the most sensible retreat square. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6. d4 d5 {Black strikes back in the center with ...d5 most frequently, 40% of the time. We'll continue by closing the center with e5. We get a pawn structure that resembles the French, except Black doesn't have the ...c5 break.} 7. e5 {We continue with d4, transposing into the main line. [%csl Gd4]} 7... Nc6 {Black continues developing with ...Nc6 around 58% of the time. We continue by developing our light-squared bishop to d3. On a side note, if Black ever plays ...Nh6, we will capture that knight.} (7... f6 {Occasionally, Black might try to strike at our center immediately with ...f6, but this simply isn't that great. It happens 12% of the time. [%csl Re5]} 8. Bd3 {We continue with Bd3 and prepare the powerful Qh5+. White already has a very comfortable advantage. [%cal Gd1h5,Rf6e5] [%csl Gh5,Re5]}) 8. Bd3 Bd7 {Black moves their bishop to d7. We quickly develop our queen and attack the vulnerable g7-pawn. This is one of the downsides of moving the dark-squared bishop early for Black.} (8... f6 {Black might try to chip away at our structure with ...f6, but this is simply too weakening. We immediately expose Black's previous move by bringing our queen out to h5! Of course, if Black were to block with ...g6, we have the winning tactic with Bxg6. [%csl Re5]} 9. Qh5+ Kf8 {Black slides the king over to f8.} 10. Nf3 {We simply develop our knight to f3, preparing O-O. With a dominant space advantage and better piece activity, White is already much better, and it is unclear how Black should even attempt to untangle. Any attempt to close the position with ...f5 can be met with g4. [%cal Ge1g1] [%csl Gg1]}) (8... f5 {Another common move players go for is the immediate ...f5, looking to grab some central space and close the position.} 9. h4 {We continue with h4, preventing the annoying ...Bg5! We'll proceed with g4, and a common idea is to bring the g-knight to f4 via h3 or e2. Our h-pawn is currently untouchable because of Qh5+ threats. We are borderline winning. [%cal Gg2g4,Gg1e2,Ge2f4] [%csl Gf4,Gg4]}) 9. Qg4 g6 {Most often, Black defends the g-pawn with ...g6. But this prompts an aggressive idea. We continue with h4! We want to play h5 and attack Black's kingside.} (9... Kf8 {Black can also defend the g-pawn with the king. This might seem somewhat awkward at first glance, but we don't actually have a great way to expose the king's positioning. [%csl Gg7]} 10. Na3 {We'll continue by developing our knight to a3. We want to control c4 and jump to c2 if needed rather than aim for b5. We'll continue to develop with Ne2 and O-O in this rich and complex position. Our dark-squared bishop can develop to e3, and our rook can go to b1. [%cal Gg1e2,Ge1g1,Ga1b1,Gc1e3,Ga3c2] [%csl Ge2,Gg1,Gb1,Ge3]}) 10. h4 h5 {Black prevents h5 by playing ...h5 and attacking our queen. We'll simply slide it back to g3. [%csl Rg4]} 11. Qg3 Nh6 {Black continues developing with ...Nh6. The knight wants to control the light squares on the kingside. [%cal Gh6f5] [%csl Gf5,Gg4]} 12. Ne2 {We are down a pawn, but have sufficient compensation with the space advantage and superior piece activity. A surprisingly common motif in these positions is the knight sacrifice on g6. If Black now chooses to jump their knight into f5, we can simply capture and proceed by bringing our knight to f4. [%cal Ge2f4,Gc1e3] [%csl Gf4,Ge3]} *

[Chapter "Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5"]
[Study "Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...d6"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/1.e4 Gotham Style Part 2 The Sicilian/04-02 Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 - Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...d6.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{We're continuing our analysis of the a3 Sicilian with 2...e6. In this study, we will be taking a look at how to respond if Black, after accepting our gambit, chooses to set up with ...d6 on move 6.}
1. e4 c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll continue with a3.} 2. a3 e6 {Black goes for ...e6. We will offer up a pawn with b4.} 3. b4 cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We recapture on b4.} 4. axb4 Bxb4 {Black challenges the authority of our gambit by capturing on b4 with the bishop. Let's attack the bishop with c3.} 5. c3 Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to e7. This is the most sensible retreat square. We establish a nice central structure by expanding with d4.} 6. d4 d6 {Black second option is to set up in a "less confrontational" way with ...d6, and it's played 22% of the time. By playing a small center approach, Black wants to remain solid and claim some space but sacrifices a lot of piece activity for it. Our plan after Bd3 is to set up with f4, Nf3 and O-O, and reply actively to Black's setup.} 7. Bd3 Nf6 {Black continues with ...Nf6, developing a piece and preparing ...O-O. We expand with f4, grabbing some central space and looking to develop our g1-knight behind the f-pawn. [%cal Ge8g8]} (7... Nc6 {This is Black's main alternative to ...Nf6. But it simply transposes after we continue with f4 and Nf3.}) 8. f4 O-O {Black continues by castling their king. We continue with our plan by developing the knight behind the pawn.} (8... Nc6 {This is Black's main alternative to castling. But it tranposes after Nf3.}) 9. Nf3 Nc6 {Black develops their other knight.} (9... b6 {Instead of the natural ...Nc6, Black can also continue with ...b6, looking to quickly fianchetto their light-squared bishop. In this case, we'll postpone castling and strike immediately with e5! Capturing on e5 is actually not advisable for Black, as we can recapture with our f-pawn and play Qc2. [%cal Gc8b7]} 10. e5 Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We continue with h4, looking to kickstart an attack on Black's castled king. Black's best response is now ...h6, against which we can look to set up a powerful battery with Bc2 and Qd3.} 11. h4 Bb7 {Black continues developing by fianchettoing their bishop instead. What could possibly go wrong?} 12. Bxh7+ {Well, we can strike with the "Greek Gift" sacrifice! After we follow it up with Ng5, Black is simply dead lost! The idea is that the queen is coming to h5. [%cal Gf3g5,Gd1h5] [%csl Gg5]}) 10. O-O {As per usual, the position is objectively equal, but we have great compensation for the sacrificed pawn and Black can easily make a mistake. We'll continue to develop with Nbd2 and look to play a timely e5. If Black pushes in the center, we should avoid trading pawns. [%cal Gb1d2,Ge4e5] [%csl Gd2,Ge5]} *

[Chapter "Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5"]
[Study "Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...Nf6"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/1.e4 Gotham Style Part 2 The Sicilian/04-03 Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5 - Black Plays 2...e6 and 6...Nf6.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{We're continuing our analysis of the a3 Sicilian with 2...e6. In this study, we will be taking a look at how to respond if Black, after accepting our gambit, chooses to play 6...Nf6.}
1. e4 c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Let's continue with a3.} 2. a3 e6 {Quite often, Black plays ...e6 on move two. Let's gambit a pawn with b4.} 3. b4 cxb4 {Most often, Black will continue by capturing the pawn on b4. We'll recapture.} 4. axb4 Bxb4 {Black captures on b4 again. We'll attack the bishop with c3. Still standard.} 5. c3 Be7 {Retreating to e7. Let's expand in the center.} 6. d4 Nf6 {Black's third option is ...Nf6, developing the kingside knight and attacking the e-pawn. It's played almost a quarter of the time. This prompts us to push e5 with tempo and grab even more space. [%csl Re4]} 7. e5 Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. Before Black castles, we have the opportunity to slide the queen to g4 and attack the undefended g7-pawn. It's not all that comfortable to defend it.} 8. Qg4 g6 {Most often, Black defends the g-pawn with ...g6. We can simply play Nf3, supporting the center and looking to develop our light-squared bishop and castle kingside. If Black now castles, we can start an attack on the kingside with h4.} (8... Kf8 {Black can also defend the g-pawn with the king.} 9. Qd1 {We'll channel our inner Stockfish by retreating the queen back to d1! This fascinating novelty keeps the queen "flexible" on the central d-file and avoids some annoying ...h5 ideas. We'll look to develop with Nf3, Bd3 and O-O. [%cal Gg1f3,Gf1d3,Ge1g1] [%csl Gf3,Gd3,Gg1]}) (8... O-O {Black can also castle, but this is simply too dangerous. Using the pin on the g-file, we move the bishop out to h6 and threaten checkmate.} 9. Bh6 g6 {Black defends against checkmate with ...g6.} 10. h4 {Don't rush to capture the f8-rook! Much stronger is h4, threatening to open up Black's king with h5. White has a comfortable advantage, a strong attack and great winning chances. [%cal Gh4h5] [%csl Gg4,Gh6,Rg8,Rg6]}) 9. Nf3 d6 {Black quickly fights back in the center with ...d6 instead.} 10. Bh6 {We move our bishop out to h6, exposing the weakened dark squares and preventing Black from castling. It is pretty difficult to give a generalized plan, but we should look to develop with Be2 or Bb5 and castle. A good rule of thumb is that we should pin Black's knight if it arrives on c6 or d7. [%cal Gf1e2,Ge2b5,Ge1g1] [%csl Gb5,Ge2,Gg1,Re8]} *

[Chapter "Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5"]
[Study "Black Plays 2...e6 and 3...d5"]
