{In this study, we will be taking a look at how to respond if Black chooses to decline our gambit with 3...d5.}
1. e4 c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll respond with a3.} 2. a3 e6 {Black goes for ...e6. Let's reply with b4.} 3. b4 d5 {Black declines the gambit by striking back in the center with ...d5, which occurs 12% of the time. This is an aggressive and a direct approach where Black wastes no time grabbing central space instead of letting us play d4. We'll continue by capturing on d5. [%csl Re4]} 4. exd5 exd5 {Most often, Black recaptures with the pawn. It happens more than 80% of the time We quickly develop our bishop to b5 and give a check.} (4... Qxd5 {Black also has the option to recapture with the queen. This is rarer with 13%, but there are still nearly 3,600 games in the database with this position. We develop our queenside knight and attack Black's queen.} 5. Nc3 Qe5+ {Most often, Black gives a check by moving the queen to e5. We block the check by developing our bishop to e2. We will look to continue developing with Nf3 and O-O. [%csl Re1]} (5... Qd8 {Black's other option is to retreat the queen to its home square, d8. We continue by developing our bishop to b2. This is convenient since we've pushed b4.} 6. Bb2 Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight.} (6... cxb4 {Similarly to the lines with ...Qe5+, Black often makes the mistake of capturing the "poisoned" b-pawn. We recapture on b4.} 7. axb4 Bxb4 {Black captures the pawn on b4 and simultaneously develops their dark-squared bishop. What could go wrong this time? Oops! We move our queen out to g4, attacking Black's bishop and the g-pawn.} 8. Qg4 Bf8 {Black sets up for the next game by retreating the bishop to f8.} 9. Qg3 {And just as Black thought they were safe, we calmly slide our queen back a square. This seemingly harmless move prepares the deadly Nb5-Nc7, and Black's position is beginning to quickly fall apart. White is simply better and will have a very pleasant game. [%cal Gc3b5,Gb5c7] [%csl Gc7,Rc7]}) 7. b5 {We close the queenside with b5. White has a slight edge, and we will continue developing with Nf3, g3, Bg2 and O-O. [%cal Gg1f3,Gg2g3,Gf1g2] [%csl Gf3,Gg3,Gg2]}) 6. Be2 cxb4 {In nearly 90% of games which reached the position after Be2, Black made the mistake of capturing the b-pawn. We recapture on b4.} 7. axb4 Bxb4 {Black captures the pawn on b4 and simultaneously develops their dark-squared bishop. We quickly jump our knight to f3 and attack Black's queen! If Black now retreats the queen to f6, we can develop our bishop to b2 and line up some nasty discovered attacks.} 8. Nf3 Qc7 {Black retreats the queen to c7 instead. We jump our knight into b5 and attack Black's queen once again!} 9. Nb5 Qd8 {Black retreats to d8.} 10. Bb2 {We develop our bishop to b2 with what's already a winning advantage. There are two main threats: We are threatening to win the rook with Bxg7, and we are threatening Be5, from where our bishop supports the c7-square for the b5-knight. We might also look to win our pawn back by capturing on a7. [%cal Gb2e5,Ge5g7,Gb5a7,Ga1a7] [%csl Ga7,Re5,Rg7,Ra7]}) 5. Bb5+ Nc6 {Black blocks the check by developing their knight to c6. Surprisingly, this is the only good way to meet the check. We develop our knight to f3 and prepare to castle the king.} (5... Bd7 {Black can also block the check by developing the bishop to d7. We move our queen to e2 and give a check to Black's king. This wins another tempo and challenges Black to choose how they will block. [%csl Rb5]} 6. Qe2+ Be7 {Most often, Black blocks the check by moving their dark-squared bishop to e7. This is, however, inaccurate.} (6... Qe7 {Black can also block with ...Qe7, offering a trade of queens. We should respond by developing our knight to c3. This move supports the b5-bishop and pressures the d5-pawn.} 7. Nc3 Qxe2+ {Black trades the queens on e2. We recapture on e2 with the g-knight. If Black now attempts to capture on b4, Bxd7+ followed by Nxd5 yields White a considerable advantage.} 8. Ngxe2 Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. We proceed by capturing on c5. We didn't want Black to take on b4. [%csl Gd5]} 9. bxc5 Bxc5 {Black recaptures on c5.} 10. d3 {White has a cozy advantage after d3, and Black will struggle to defend their isolated d5-pawn for the rest of the game. We can continue developing with O-O and Bg5, looking to further undermine the defense of d5. The natural-looking ...d4 can now conveniently be met with Na4. [%cal Gc1g5,Ge1g1] [%csl Gg5,Gg1,Rd5]}) 7. Bb2 {We continue with Bb2, targeting the g7-pawn and immediately punishing Black for moving the bishop from f8! With the combined threats of Bxg7 and bxc5, White has a great initiative and an advantage. Apart from those threats, we also have easy development with Nf3 and O-O. If ...Nf6, we can take. [%cal Gb4c5,Gg1f3,Ge1g1] [%csl Gf3,Gg1,Gb2,Rg7,Rc5]}) 6. Nf3 Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight now. We'll castle in response, with our rook ready to come to e1 if needed. That in conjuction with Qe2 could be quite strong if Black isn't careful.} (6... cxb4 {Many players find it tempting to capture the pawn on b4. We castle our king and are now looking to quickly bring our rook to the e-file. It is now inadvisable for Black to capture the a3-pawn, since Re1+ would come with a lot of pressure and Nd4 and c4 ideas.} 7. O-O Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight. We quickly move our rook to e1 and give a check. If Black now blocks the check with the light-squared bishop, we can jump our knight into d4.} 8. Re1+ Be7 {Black blocks the check by moving their dark-squared bishop to e7. We capture the pawn on b4, making use of the fact that both Black's c6-knight and e7-bishop are pinned.} 9. axb4 O-O {Black castles their king. This move unpins both of Black's minor pieces and opens up an attack on the b4-pawn. We'll defend and support the b4-pawn with c3. [%csl Rb4]} 10. c3 Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop to g4 and pins our knight. [%csl Rf3]} 11. d4 {We expand in the center with d4. The computer, once again, claims equality, but with all pieces still on the board, and with the structural imbalance on the queenside, the position is still quite "lively". We'll look to continue developing with Bf4 and Nbd2. [%cal Gc1f4,Gb1d2] [%csl Gf4,Gd2]}) 7. O-O Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop to e7. Now we can capture on c5, forcing Black to potentially lose a tempo recapturing. And there is simply no reason not to recapture as the simple d4 will secure us a long-term advantage with an extra pawn.} 8. bxc5 Bxc5 {Black recaptures the pawn on c5. We begin an initiative by expanding with d4 anyway and attacking Black's bishop.} 9. d4 Be7 {Black retreats to e7. We jump our knight into e5, adding an attacker to Black's pinned c6-knight.} 10. Ne5 Bd7 {Black develops the light-squared bishop and supports the c6-knight. We will trade for the bishop on d7. This leaves us with the advantageous bishop pair. [%csl Gc6]} 11. Nxd7 Qxd7 {Black recaptures with the queen.} 12. Nd2 {We continue with Nd2, looking to jump our knight to the f3-square. Our rook can develop to e1 and a thematic c4-break is on the cards. If Black plays ...a6 now, we'll capture the knight because of the d4-pawn. Otherwise, we'll look to save the bishop. [%cal Gd2f3,Gf1e1,Gc2c4] [%csl Gf3,Ge1,Gc4]} *

