{We're continuing our analysis of the a3 Sicilian with 2...e6. In this study, we will be taking a look at how to respond if Black chooses to decline our gambit with 3...b6.}
1. e4 c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll reply with a3.} 2. a3 e6 {Black goes for ...e6. Let's strike with b4.} 3. b4 b6 {Black declines the gambit with ...b6 8% of the time. This move supports the c-pawn and prepares to fianchetto the light-squared bishop. We'll take on c5. If the bishop recaptures, we can play actively with d4. In general, the opening of the b-file is beneficial for us in these setups. [%cal Gc8b7]} 4. bxc5 {We continue by capturing on c5.} 4... bxc5 {Black recaptures with the b-pawn instead. We'll simply develop our knight now.} 5. Nf3 Bb7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop and targets our e-pawn. We'll develop our queenside knight and defend the attacked pawn. [%csl Re4]} (5... Nc6 {A close second in popularity is ...Nc6. We develop our bishop to b5, looking to capture on c6 and ruin Black's pawn structure.} 6. Bb5 Bb7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop and supports the c6-knight. We'll castle. If Black now attacks our bishop with ...a6, we can simply retreat it to a4.} 7. O-O Nf6 {Black attacks the e-pawn by developing their kingside knight. [%csl Re4]} 8. d3 {Remember not to push e5! Instead, we should support the pawn with d3 for a more stable structure. The position is, again, objectively equal, but extremely rich and relatively double-edged. We'll look to finish our development with Bf4 or Bg5, Nbd2 and Re1. [%cal Gc1f4,Gb1d2,Gf1e1,Gf4g5] [%csl Gf4,Gd2,Ge1,Gg5]}) (5... d5 {Black might attempt to immediately strike in the center with ...d5, but this allows us to get a pretty comfortable position. We'll take for starters. [%csl Re4]} 6. exd5 exd5 {Black recaptures on d5. Since Black has moved the d-pawn, we can develop with tempo and give a check with the bishop on b5.} 7. Bb5+ Bd7 {Black blocks the check and offers a trade of bishops. We move our queen to e2, giving a check and supporting the b5-bishop. If Black now blocks the check with their queen, we have the powerful Ne5!} 8. Qe2+ Be7 {Black blocks the check with their bishop instead.} 9. O-O {We castle our king. White now has a pleasant advantage, and we will follow up with Re1, making it difficult for Black to castle. Our queenside bishop can develop to b2, and our queenside knight can develop to c3. [%cal Gf1e1,Gc1b2,Gb1c3] [%csl Ge1,Gb2,Gc3]}) 6. Nc3 Nf6 {Black attacks the e-pawn once more by developing their kingside knight. In this instance, we can push e5 and counterattack the knight. Pushing d3 would block our light-squared bishop. [%csl Re4]} (6... Nc6 {Black can also develop the queenside knight.} 7. Bc4 {We'll develop our light-squared bishop to c4. The computer claims equality, but with only the b-pawns having been traded, the position is still extremely interesting. We'll look to finish our development with O-O, d3, Rb1 and Bf4. [%cal Gd2d3,Ge1g1,Ga1b1,Gc1f4] [%csl Gd3,Gg1,Gb1,Gf4]}) 7. e5 Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. Instead of trading the knights, we can win a tempo on Black's bishop. [%csl Rc3]} 8. Rb1 Nxc3 {Before defending or moving the b7-bishop, Black throws in a capture on c3.} 9. dxc3 {We recapture on c3. As per usual, the position is objectively equal, but incredibly dynamic and complex. We'll look to continue developing with Be2, O-O and Bf4 once Black moves away the bishop or defends it. [%cal Gf1e2,Ge1g1,Gc1f4] [%csl Ge2,Gg1,Gf4,Rb7]} *

[Chapter "Black Plays 2...e6 or 2...e5"]
