{We're continuing our analysis of the a3 Sicilian and taking a look at how we should respond if Black chooses to play ...e5 on move two.}
1. e4 c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll respond with a3.} 2. a3 e5 {You should face ...e5 only 2% of the time. This adds control to the d4-square but weakens the light squares and the d7-pawn severely. ...e5 is actually too weakening and we no longer have to gambit at all! Instead, we'll look to continue in "Vienna" style with Bc4, d3, f4, Nc3 and so on. [%csl Rd7]} 3. Bc4 Nc6 {Black continues by developing their queenside knight, and will look to set up with ...Nf6, ...d6 and ...Be7. Regardless of Black's move order, we'll play d3. This move supports the e-pawn, solidifies our center and prepares f4.} 4. d3 Nf6 {Black develops their other knight. We continue with our plan by developing the queenside knight.} 5. Nc3 Be7 {Black develops their bishop to e7 and prepares to castle kingside.} 6. f4 {We expand with f4 and look to develop our knight to f3. This does weaken our kingside, but Black has no immediate way to expose the weakness of the e1-h4 diagonal. This is now a Two Knights Vienna, with a misplaced c-pawn for Black, and a useful addition of a3 for White. Nf3 and O-O are next. [%cal Gg1f3,Ge1g1] [%csl Gf3,Gg1,Gf4,Re5]} *

[Chapter "Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2"]
[Study "Black Plays 2...d6, Main Lines"]
