{Welcome to the ninth chapter! In this chapter, we will be concluding our analysis of the a3 Sicilian and taking a look at how to respond if Black chooses to play ...d6, ...d5, ...Nf6 or ...b6 on move two. In this particular study, we will be taking a look at the flexible and non-confrontational 2...d6.}
1. e4 c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We will respond with a3.} 2. a3 d6 {Black plays ...d6 on move two 22% of the time, and it happened in more than 750,000 games. Black's 2...d6 controls the e5- square, supports the c-pawn and activates their light-squared bishop. Against this, we will play b4, looking to undermine Black's control of the d4-square.} 3. b4 Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn 14% of the time. We develop our queenside knight and defend it. [%csl Re4]} (3... cxb4 {Black can also capture on b4, but this is inadvisable since Black relinquishes control over the important d4-square. This happens almost half the time. We'll respond by recapturing on b4.} 4. axb4 Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn. We develop our queenside knight and defend the attacked pawn. [%csl Re4]} 5. Nc3 g6 {Most often, Black looks to set up in "Dragon" style with ...g6 and ...Bg7. We expand in the center with d4, naturally. [%cal Gf8g7]} (5... e6 {Black can also look to develop with ...e6 and ...Be7. Against 5...e6, we shouldn't rush with d4, since Black would be able to meet us with a quick ...d5. Instead, we'll prioritize developing our kingside with Nf3, Be2 and castling. [%cal Gf8e7]} 6. Nf3 Be7 {Black develops the bishop to e7 and prepares to castle kingside. We continue with our plan by developing the light-squared bishop.} 7. Be2 O-O {Black castles their king.} 8. O-O {We castle our king as well. With the space advantage, semi-open a-file and better piece activity, White has an enjoyable advantage. We will look to expand with d4, and our dark-squared bishop can develop to b2 or d2. [%cal Gd2d4] [%csl Gd4]}) 6. d4 Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll play h3 in order to prevent any annoying pins with ...Bg4.} 7. h3 O-O {Black castles their king.} 8. Be3 {We develop our bishop to e3, supporting the d-pawn. With the space advantage, dominant central presence and the semi-open a-file, White has an advantage. We will look to continue developing with Bd3, Nf3 and O-O. [%cal Gg1f3,Gf1d3,Ge1g1] [%csl Gf3,Gd3,Gg1]}) 4. Nc3 Nc6 {Black develops their knight to c6 and adds an attacker to the b-pawn now. Instead of trying to defend it, we will simply capture on c5. [%csl Rb4]} (4... g6 {Black chooses the fianchetto approach. We will slide our rook to b1 prophylactically. Also, we are planning to open up the b-file. [%cal Gf8g7]} 5. Rb1 Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll continue by capturing on c5 and opening up that b-file. Our rook is coming to light now since it's harder for Black's bishop to develop.} 6. bxc5 dxc5 {Black recaptures on c5.} 7. Bc4 {This position is very similar to the main continuation, and the same idea applies. We will look to continue developing with Nge2, O-O and d3. The important nuance is that we should develop our kingside knight to e2 (and not f3), in order to support the c3-knight, and to be able to meet any ...Bg4 with f3. [%cal Ge1g1,Gd2d3,Gg1e2] [%csl Gd3,Gg1,Ge2]}) 5. bxc5 dxc5 {Black recaptures on c5.} 6. Bc4 {We develop our light-squared bishop to c4. The open b-file and the questionable placement of Black's c8-bishop for the rest of the game makes our prospects nice. We will look to continue developing with d3, Nf3, O-O and Rb1. [%cal Gd2d3,Ga1b1,Gg1f3,Ge1g1] [%csl Gd3,Gb1,Gf3,Gg1]} *

[Chapter "Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2"]
[Study "Black Pushes The Early 2...d5"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/1.e4 Gotham Style Part 2 The Sicilian/05-02 Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2 - Black Pushes The Early 2...d5.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{In this study, we will be taking a look at how to respond if Black chooses to meet our 2. a3 with the "Scandinavian-esque" 2...d5.}
1. e4 c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Let's go for a3.} 2. a3 d5 {Black chooses to strike in the center with ...d5. This happens 4% of the time. No reason not to capture and leave Black with a strong center. [%csl Re4]} 3. exd5 Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5 77% of the time. This is now the Scandinavian Defense, but with the inclusion of a3 and c5. We don't have to rush with Nc3 and can develop to f3 first.} (3... Nf6 {Some players try to avoid losing the lead in development by attacking the pawn with 3...Nf6. It occurs 20% of the time. We will try to develop rapidly as well by giving a check on b5 with the bishop. [%csl Rd5]} 4. Bb5+ Bd7 {Most often, Black blocks the check by developing the bishop to d7. There is little merit to retreating here, so we'll trade. [%csl Rb5]} (4... Nbd7 {Black can also block the check with the b8-knight. We'll develop our queenside knight and defend the d-pawn.} 5. Nc3 a6 {Expect to see ...a6 at some point. We'll retreat to e2. [%csl Rb5]} 6. Be2 Nb6 {Black jumps the knight into b6, opening up a triple attack on the weak d-pawn, which looks problematic. We'll develop our knight to f3, though. We cannot hold onto the pawn, so we'll look to gain a significant lead in development while Black spends time winning it back. [%csl Rd5]} 7. Nf3 Nbxd5 {Black captures on d5, restoring material balance. Let's trade once.} 8. Nxd5 Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen.} 9. O-O {We castle our king. With a clear lead in development, White now simply has a better position. Our plan is to expand in the center and on the queenside with c4, d4 and b4. [%cal Gc2c4,Gb2b4,Gd2d4] [%csl Gb4,Gc4,Gd4]}) 5. Bxd7+ Qxd7 {Black recaptures with the queen. There are two attackers on the pawn now. The only way to save it is c4. [%csl Rd5]} 6. c4 e6 {Black plays ...e6, looking to chip away at our pawn wedge. That's why we will slide the queen to e2 and pin Black's e6-pawn. The queen is now more active and participates in defending our pawn structure. [%csl Rd5,Rc4]} 7. Qe2 Be7 {Black develops the bishop to e7, unpinning the pawn and preparing to castle kingside. We have to capture on e6 at this point. [%cal Ge8g8] [%csl Rd5]} 8. dxe6 fxe6 {Black recaptures with the pawn as to keep the queens on the board. This is another reason behind Qe2; if Black doesn't want to trade queens down a pawn, they have to worsen their pawn structure!} 9. Nf3 {We'll simply develop our knight to f3. With an extra pawn and a clear target on e6, White is simply better and will have a relatively easy game. We can look to continue developing with O-O, d3 and Nc3. [%cal Ge1g1,Gd2d3,Gb1c3] [%csl Gg1,Gd3,Gc3,Re6]}) 4. Nf3 Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight. Let's develop with tempo on the queen now.} (4... Nf6 {Black often develops the kingside knight, but this is inaccurate! We can take the initiative by developing our knight to c3 and attacking Black's queen.} 5. Nc3 Qd8 {Black retreats the queen to its home square, d8.} 6. d4 {We strike in the center with d4! White now has a pleasant, active position with easy development. We will look to develop with Be3, and in many cases Qe2 and O-O-O. If Black captures on d4, we should recapture with the knight. [%cal Gc1e3,Gd1e2,Ge1c1] [%csl Ge3,Ge2,Gc1]}) 5. Nc3 Qd8 {Black goes back to d8. We can continue in an aggressive fashion, this time activating the bishop to b5. We are threatening to capture and disrupt Black's pawn structure.} 6. Bb5 Bd7 {That's why Black develops the bishop to d7 and supports the c6-knight. How can we continue being aggressive? We can jump to e4 and attack Black's c5-pawn now! [%csl Gc6]} 7. Ne4 e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and defends the pawn. [%csl Gc5]} 8. c3 {We'll continue with c3, supporting the b4- and d4-squares. This was another idea behind Ne4. White has a slight edge, and we'll look to continue with O-O and d4 or even b4. [%cal Gd2d4,Gb2b4,Ge1g1] [%csl Gd4,Gb4,Gg1]} *

[Chapter "Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2"]
[Study "Black Plays 2...b6 or 2...Nf6"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/1.e4 Gotham Style Part 2 The Sicilian/05-03 Black's Rare Sidelines on Move 2 - Black Plays 2...b6 or 2...Nf6.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{In this study, we will be taking a look at how to respond if Black chooses to meet our 2. a3 with 2...b6 or 2...Nf6.}
1. e4 c5 {Black goes for 1...c5. Let's go for a3.} 2. a3 b6 {Black plays ...b6 only 1% of the time, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and supporting the c5-pawn. We don't really have to go b4 now. Instead, we'll quickly strike in the center with d4! [%cal Gc8b7]} (2... Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn 2% of the time. It's logical to push e5 and counterattack the knight. [%csl Re4]} 3. e5 Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll strike in the center with d4! We don't have to play b4 like usual here.} 4. d4 cxd4 {Black captures the pawn on d4. We recapture on d4 and attack Black's knight.} 5. Qxd4 e6 {Black plays ...e6, defending the knight and opening up the f8- bishop. We develop our knight to f3 in a standard manner. [%csl Gd5]} 6. Nf3 Nc6 {Black will attack our queen with ...Nc6 at some point. We'll retreat the queen to its home square, d1. From there it's safe and still has potential. [%csl Rd4]} 7. Qd1 d6 {By far the most common is ...d6, since Black is looking to get rid of our e-pawn. But we won't play it slow and give Black what they want. We start an initiative by attacking Black's knight with c4! If Black now retreats the knight to c7, we can simply continue with Bf4. [%csl Re5]} (7... Qc7 {Black moves the queen to c7 and adds an attacker to the e5-pawn. We attack Black's knight with c4! The line that follows is pretty much completely forced. If Black now retreats the knight to b6, we can simply continue with Bf4. [%csl Re5]} 8. c4 Nde7 {Black retreats the knight to e7. It's important to develop the knight to c3 and ignore the attack on the e-pawn. You will see why.} 9. Nc3 Nxe5 {Black captures the pawn on e5. We can jump our knight into b5, attacking Black's queen.} 10. Nb5 Nxf3+ {Black captures on f3 with check. We'll recapture with the queen. [%csl Re1]} 11. Qxf3 Qe5+ {Black moves the queen to e5 and gives a check to our king. This is probably what you will face. We block the check by moving our light-squared bishop to e2. We are now threatening the devastating Bf4. [%csl Re1]} 12. Be2 Ng6 {Black jumps the knight into g6, defending against the threat. We slide our queen to g3, forcing a queen trade. Black cannot decline the trade, since our queen and knight coordinate towards the c7-square! [%csl Rf4]} 13. Qg3 Qxg3 {Black trades queens on g3. We recapture with the h-pawn. We are now threatening Nc7+.} 14. hxg3 Kd8 {Black defends c7 with the king. [%csl Gc7]} 15. Bd2 {We are down a pawn for the moment, but Black's position is simply dreadful. Not only are we likely to win the pawn back, but our pieces are also super active and we have a significant lead in development. According to the engine, Black's only try is now ...b6, to which we will respond with Bf3 and Nxa7, winning our pawn back. [%cal Ge1c1,Gb7b6,Ge2f3] [%csl Gc1]}) 8. c4 Nb6 {Black retreats the knight to b6 instead. We'll attack Black's queen with Bg5 now! If Black now carelessly plays ...Be7, we can simply win a pawn after Bxe7 and exd6.} 9. Bg5 Qc7 {So, Black moves the queen to c7. Let's capture the pawn on d6 and finally relieve the tension.} 10. exd6 Bxd6 {Black recaptures with the bishop. We'll develop our knight to c3, threatening the deadly Nb5.} 11. Nc3 a6 {Black prevents Nb5 with ...a6. But we'll attack Black's bishop by jumping our knight into e4 instead! [%csl Rb5]} 12. Ne4 Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to e7. The initiative doesn't stop! We attack the b6-knight with c5.} 13. c5 Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5, naturally. We will trade the bishops on e7. If Black recaptures with the queen, we can jump our knight into d6.} 14. Bxe7 Kxe7 {Black recaptures with the king instead.} 15. Be2 {We'll develop our bishop to e2, preparing to castle kingside. The c5-pawn is a thorn in Black's position, the d6-square is a potential outpost for our knight, and Black's king on e7 is misplaced. White is better. [%cal Ge1g1] [%csl Gg1]}) 3. d4 cxd4 {Black captures on d4. We'll recapture on d4 with our queen.} 4. Qxd4 Nc6 {Black develops the knight to c6 and attacks our queen, which is expected. We slide the queen back to d3. Wasting this tempo is not the end of the world, since Black has committed to b7 with the bishop, which is a bad square. [%csl Rd4]} 5. Qd3 Bb7 {Black fianchettoes the light-squared bishop. It's not all that active, though. We'll simply develop our knight to c3.} 6. Nc3 Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight.} 7. Nf3 {We develop our other knight to f3. The dominant central presence and Black's misplaced light-squared bishop give White a pleasant edge. We'll look to develop our dark-squared bishop next and castle queenside. [%cal Gc1e3,Ge3f4,Gf4g5,Ge1c1] [%csl Gg5,Ge3,Gf4,Gc1]} *
